The main saving is a fast path for the common case where the field's base-type size equals `sizeof(T)`.

- **Fast path:** on little-endian hosts, the bytes are copied in with a single `memcpy`, which compiles to one load. This gives the same bits as the original shift-and-OR loop.
- **Fallback:** if the sizes differ or the host is big-endian, the original byte loop runs, now reading through a raw pointer to the vector's data.
- **Single type lookup:** `GetType()` is called once instead of twice. This assumes `GetType()` has no side effects, which is the case for a plain accessor.
- **Unchanged behavior:** the out-of-range check and the invalid-value return are the same as before.

```cpp
template <typename T>
T FieldBase::GetValue(const FIT_UINT8 fieldArrayIndex) const
{
    const FIT_UINT8 typeNum = GetType() & FIT_BASE_TYPE_NUM_MASK;
    const FIT_UINT8 size = baseTypeSizes[typeNum];
    if (fieldArrayIndex >= (FIT_UINT8)values.size())
    {
        return *reinterpret_cast<const T*>(baseTypeInvalids[typeNum]);
    }

    const FIT_BYTE* p = &values[0] + (static_cast<size_t>(fieldArrayIndex) * size);

#if defined(__BYTE_ORDER__) && defined(__ORDER_LITTLE_ENDIAN__) && (__BYTE_ORDER__ == __ORDER_LITTLE_ENDIAN__)
    if (size == sizeof(T))
    {
        T rv;
        std::memcpy(&rv, p, sizeof(T));
        return rv;
    }
#endif

    T rv = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++)
    {
        rv |= static_cast<T>(p[i]) << (i * 8);
    }

    return rv;
}
```

